Subject: [PATCH] projectM TV: lines as quads that scale with the render size

projectM issue #682. MilkDrop draws waveforms, custom waves, shape outlines and motion vectors as
1 px GL lines (thick ones four times, offset by up to a pixel), so their share of the picture
changes with the render size and a preset that feeds its image back looked different at every
quality level. projectm_opengl_set_line_reference_height() draws them as quads instead: one
instance per segment with miter joins, 1 px wide at and below the reference height and in
proportion above it (below it, thinner lines faded by their width made such presets go dark, so
they are drawn as at the reference height). Edges are hard like MilkDrop's GL lines (the quad is
the band itself, so the rasterizer resolves pixel-border ties; on GLES a 1/64 px offset makes those
ties land on the row Mali's GL lines light); projectm_opengl_set_line_antialiasing() opts into a
one-pixel anti-aliased edge. Thick lines are the thin line drawn four times with MilkDrop's
offsets (a pixel for the main wave, half a pixel for custom waves and shape outlines, scaled like
the width). Waveform dots stay GL points with a scaled size. If the driver rejects the line
shaders, GL lines are drawn. 0, the default, keeps the GL lines.
Unlike the issue's suggestion, waveforms also get miter joins and flat ends, not round joins and
caps: round joins overlap the segments and blend joint pixels twice (bright beads on translucent
or additive waves), at 1-2 px the join shape is sub-pixel, and MilkDrop's lines have none.
---
